<Chapter id="why" label="So why did they win?" />

## So why did they win?

The Morning Ledger has already found the secret of success:

<WinnerStory />

It sounds almost reasonable. That is how the trick works.

The result came first. The explanation came second. Color, corners and edges never touched
the game.

Run it again. Maybe a new winner. Maybe the same one twice. The paper never prints
“chance.” It simply finds another reason.

Remember the headline we started with? How many reasons did you add before seeing the
mechanism?

<Chapter id="line-them-up" label="Line them up" />

## Line them up

A hundred circles, all different sizes, scattered around the room. You cannot read that.
Nobody can.

So sort them. Smallest to largest, and drop each one into a bin — a pile for the near-broke,
a pile for the comfortable, a pile for the rich.

<!--
  WIDGET: DistributionStage (R5, owner review 2026-07-08: "one stage for both sorting and
  log scale") — BinsStage + AxisStage merged into one stepper: room → sorted → binned →
  multiplying ruler. Built FROM the same circles the reader watched trade (latest logged
  run; the seeded reveal room as fallback). Bin width is a reader-visible choice, not
  nature's. Original circle coloring; no shape vocabulary needed here.
-->
<Deferred
  load={() => import('$lib/widgets/distribution/DistributionStage.svelte')}
  label="distribution stage"
  reserve="34rem"
/>

Now it is a shape: a chart whose bars are the people you just watched. (Where I cut the
lines between the piles is a choice, not nature's — move them and the picture shifts a
little.) And the shape leans hard: a crowd of the poor piled on one side, a thin tail
reaching out the other — and far past everyone, alone, the winner.

The linear chart has a problem, though: to fit the winner on screen, everyone else gets
crushed into a sliver. So the last step changes the ruler — each step *multiplies* by ten
instead of adding. The crowd opens up, and a gap between circles stops meaning how much
more; it means how many <em>times</em> more.

(One honest catch: you cannot multiply your way up from zero, so a circle with nothing has
no spot on that ruler. The truly empty sit in their own pile, off to the side.)

