<Chapter id="gini" label="Measure the room" />

## Measure the room

A histogram is a picture. Sometimes you need a single number instead — to compare two
rooms, or to watch one room drift.

Start with Gini.

<!--
  WIDGET: GiniStage (R5, owner storyboard 2026-07-08) — the Gini built from first
  principles: equal circles merge one by one into a running total (the diagonal); keep the
  graph, deal an unequal teaching room (Gini ≈ 0.5), sort, merge again (the sagging Lorenz
  curve); hatch the gap; then a slider bends parametric Lorenz curves (L = x^k) for
  intuition. Axes are the same scale, exactly square. TOY discipline: slider rooms are
  parametric, not simulation output; the number shown is the discrete Gini of the twelve
  on-screen circles. Beat 21 discipline: introduced only for comparing whole rooms; no
  per-trade Gini claims (C5).
-->
<Deferred
  load={() => import('$lib/widgets/distribution/GiniStage.svelte')}
  label="Gini stage"
  reserve="34rem"
/>

Zero: everyone holds the same. One is the theoretical limit: as the room grows, one owner
and everyone else at nothing gets closer and closer to it. The room you ran earlier has its
own number — and from here on you will see it on every meter.

But Gini is not the question I most want to ask. I want to know how many people are still
meaningfully in the game.

### Four people. How many participants?

Four people enter with four coins each. Move the coins yourself. The circles keep showing
wealth. The black box gives you one number. What arrangements make it rise? What makes it
fall?

<Deferred
  load={() => import('$lib/widgets/distribution/EffectiveParticipantsStage.svelte')}
  label="effective participants stage"
  reserve="48rem"
/>

The box reports **effective participants**: the number of equal holders that would produce
the same concentration of wealth.

Four equal people give 4. Two equal fortunes while the other two hold nothing gives 2. One
owner holding all sixteen coins gives 1. It is an equivalent count, not a headcount: nobody disappears when the
number falls. Their capacity to matter does.

<details class="claim-note">
  <summary>Show the formula</summary>
  <p>If each person's wealth share is <em>sᵢ</em>, the same-owner chance is
  <em>Σsᵢ²</em>. Effective participants is its reciprocal: <em>1 / Σsᵢ²</em>. This is the
  inverse Herfindahl concentration index.</p>
</details>

There is one more number. A trade can keep happening after the poorer player has almost
nothing left to risk. Counting trades would call that activity. Counting the money moved
tells a different story.

One **measurement round** is one trade per person on average. Add the stakes won in those
ordinary trades, then divide by all the wealth in the room. The answer is **ordinary
turnover**, measured in roomfuls. A turnover of 0.30 means that round moved wealth equal to
30% of the room. Later, when levies appear, their transfers will never be counted here.

<Chapter id="crowd" label="More of them" />

## More of them

You have seen the room. Now use all three measurements on a crowd.

<!--
  WIDGET: CrowdRun — a much larger fresh population (1,000 agents, 2M trades) runs the same
  fair trades; the log-ruled histogram forms live. Gini, effective participants, and
  ordinary turnover are recorded from the same state. One measurement round is N trades;
  levy flow is absent and cannot contaminate turnover.
-->
<Deferred load={() => import('$lib/widgets/crowd/CrowdRun.svelte')} label="crowd run" reserve="26rem" />

The histogram tells you where the money sits. Gini tells you how far the room has bent from
equality. Effective participants tells you how many equal holders the concentration is
equivalent to. Turnover tells you whether the coin is still moving meaningful money.

No one number is the truth. Together they let the room answer more than one question.

<Chapter id="where-it-ends" label="Where it ends" />

## Where it ends

So where does this go, if no one ever stops it?

This part is a theorem, not just an animation. Keep the room finite. Keep every trade fair,
every coin even. Then let it run — not a hundred thousand trades, but without end.

<!--
  WIDGET: TimeLapse — time accelerates; the tail thins until one circle holds everything the
  pixels can show. Picks up the reveal room where it stopped; a fresh room ends the same way
  with a different owner.
-->
<Deferred load={() => import('$lib/widgets/ending/TimeLapse.svelte')} label="time lapse" reserve="30rem" />

With probability one, it ends with a single owner. Everyone else goes to zero. Chance chooses
the owner, and chance may choose the same one twice — but always, in the end, exactly one.

Two cautions, because they carry the honesty of the whole piece:

- "Without end" is doing the work. This is the limit, not a date. For any finite number
  of trades the room can become deeply concentrated, but it does not literally reach one
  owner. The time-lapse shows a destination, not a deadline.
- The path can wobble. A poorer circle can win, and the room can briefly grow fairer,
  even as the long process approaches one owner.

<details class="claim-note">
  <summary>Show the theorem source</summary>
  <p>The finite-agent convergence result is proved in <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.01485">Börgers &amp; Greengard (2023)</a>.</p>
</details>

<Chapter id="dial" label="Your hand on the dial" />

## Your hand on the dial

So that is the game as written: fair coin, fixed stake, one ending.

Now you get a knob.

The stake — the slice each trade puts on the table — has been fixed this whole time. Take
hold of it. Turn it down, and each trade risks almost nothing. Turn it up, and each trade is
a gamble for a real chunk.

<!--
  WIDGET: StakeDial — dragging the stake starts a fresh room at the chosen value. Its Gini,
  effective-participant count, and ordinary turnover share one N-trade measurement clock.
  GATE(β timing): the qualitative split is safe — asymptotic concentration is the same for
  every fixed β in (0,1) (C4); the *rate* is shown as what the live runs do, not a precise
  law. Do NOT state a precise timing relationship (e.g. a formula in β or N) until the
  confirmatory grid across N, β, seeds lands (research/baseline.md). The widget's own
  footnote says "what these runs did — not a law."
-->
<Deferred load={() => import('$lib/widgets/dial/StakeDial.svelte')} label="stake dial" reserve="28rem" />

Watch what changes — and what refuses to. The Gini shows concentration, effective
participants shows how broad the field remains, and turnover shows how much wealth still
moves in an ordinary round.

Turn the stake down and the collapse drags: the room looks fair for a long, comfortable
while. Turn it up and the winner arrives fast and brutal. The speed is yours.

The ending is not. Any stake above nothing, given enough time, still empties into a single
circle. You can change how fast the room falls. You do not get to vote on whether it falls.

(Set the stake to exactly zero and the room freezes — but only because then nobody is
trading at all. Nothing at stake, nothing happens.)

<Chapter id="stop-it" label="Now you try to stop it" />

## Now you try to stop it

Reading about it is one thing. Try to fix it yourself.

Here is a live room, falling the usual way. Your job is not to punish the largest holder.
It is to keep participation open. Point at a large holder and collect a quarter of its
wealth. Every dollar you collect returns to the room in equal shares.

<!--
  WIDGET: TaxGame — the live room keeps trading while the reader taps; tapping a circle
  levies a quarter of its current wealth and shares it back equally; the tapped circle
  flinches (display affect only). This is a manual WEALTH levy (taxes current wealth, not
  income) — named in the widget copy, C13.
  DESIGN GUARD honored: the pace is NOT tuned so the reader provably cannot win — a diligent
  hand genuinely can hold the room (robot-player tuning notes in the build log). Whether a
  fast enough hand can keep a room fair is left as the real, open question the prose asks.
-->
As you play, watch the room keep trading while you work. Can a fast enough hand preserve broad
participation, or does the room outpace the taps? The game will report what your attempt did.

<Deferred load={() => import('$lib/widgets/tax/TaxGame.svelte')} label="tax game" reserve="30rem" />

Pointing is the game, so this intervention is targeted: you choose one holder. The rule
coming next is not an automated version of your hand. It is a different rule. Nobody is
selected. The same percentage applies to every fortune, the pieces enter one pool, and the
pool returns equally to everyone.

<!--
  BUILD NOTE (2026-07-06, M8) — the formerly gated middle, now built to the storyboard with
  claim discipline intact:
    20  structural levy lesson: LevyLesson keeps trading off and follows the proportional
        collection, common pool, equal return, and net result as separate reversible states.
    21  Gini — shipped in Chapter III (GiniStage), rooms-comparison only.
    22  progressive levy designer — ships inside the sandbox (M9), as a toy, no empirical claim.
    23  outcome map — finite-run grid + a chosen Gini-0.5 contour; the smooth curve is labeled
        FITTED (τ ≈ c·β²). Neither the contour nor the fit is described as a phase boundary.
-->

<Chapter id="levy" label="Put the levy in the rules" />

## Put the levy in the rules

Your hands were always going to tire. So put a structural rule in the room: a proportional
wealth levy plus an equal social return, applied on its own clock.

First freeze the trading. Four fortunes are enough to see every piece move.

<Deferred load={() => import('$lib/widgets/tax/LevyLesson.svelte')} label="levy lesson" reserve="42rem" />

Collection alone would shrink every circle by the same proportion and leave every wealth
share unchanged. The equal return is what changes the shares. Below-average fortunes receive
more than they contributed; an exactly average fortune breaks even; above-average fortunes
contribute net. The room keeps every coin.

<Chapter id="tax-against-trade" label="Tax against trade" />

## Tax against trade

Now turn trading back on. To isolate the new rule, write one fresh random script of partners
and coin tosses and give it to two rooms. One gets only the trades. The other gets the same
trades plus the shared counterforce.

<Deferred load={() => import('$lib/widgets/tax/MatchedRooms.svelte')} label="matched room comparison" reserve="38rem" />

That comparison says what happened under one disclosed finite protocol. It does not choose a
real tax code, and it does not promise the same numbers on a fresh random run. It isolates one
thing: what the levy plus equal return changed while luck stayed matched.

One pair is evidence, not a landscape. So measure many. Put stake on one axis and levy rate
on the other. Every point is one finite
experiment under the same protocol; its color records the measured outcome. You fill in the
map.

<!--
  WIDGET: PhaseDiagram (legacy component name) — the reader
  runs live rooms (SandboxWorld, fresh dice, ~2.3 s each) with snap-to-grid stake/tax dials;
  each independent finished room contributes its complete outcome bundle to the shared
  evidence store and paints its cell with measured tail-mean Gini. After three
  plays, "fill in the rest" completes the 10×13 grid live in-page (two fixed seeds per
  remaining cell, chunked under a frame budget; cached per session). White contour = chosen
  finite-run Gini 0.5 outcome. Dashed curve = FITTED τ ≈ c·β². Neither is phase evidence.
-->
<Deferred load={() => import('$lib/widgets/phase/PhaseDiagram.svelte')} label="outcome map" reserve="42rem" />

If you fill the map, it adds a chosen Gini 0.5 contour and a square-law fit. The contour is
not a border between two proven phases. The fit asks a narrower question: how does the levy
needed for the same measured outcome change as the stake changes?

<Chapter id="verdict" label="So — merit, or math?" />

## So — merit, or math?

The coin is still fair.

It never saw talent, effort, inheritance, power — or even a name. Money did not buy better
odds. It bought tosses it could survive. Enough money kept one player at the table while everyone
else's chance to make a meaningful bet disappeared.

Left alone, the fair game closed itself. Fewer people could put meaningful wealth at risk,
and less wealth moved when they traded.

Then one shared counterforce kept the field open: collect the same percentage from every
fortune, put it in one pool, and return that pool equally. The coin stayed fair. More people
stayed able to play.

This room cannot tell me why any real person is rich. I threw that world away on purpose. It
can tell me something narrower: talent and effort were not required to build the trap.

So this is not an argument against merit. It is a warning about the field merit enters.

**If merit is supposed to win, keep the game open long enough for merit to play.**

I am done touching the controls.

The machine is yours. Break my argument.

## The sandbox

Everything you have seen was this one machine wearing different costumes. Here it is with
every dial exposed: people, stake, levy rate, levy timing — and the room itself answers to
your finger. It begins equal, with fresh dice. Effective participants is the first reading;
Gini is one button away. Tap anyone, change anything, and see what it takes.

